const faqSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {"@type":"Question","name":"What is a machinery mover and what motor does it use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A machinery mover (also called a machine skate, roller skate, or equipment mover) is a low-profile platform on wheels used to transport heavy machinery, CNC machines, injection molds, and industrial equipment within a factory. Electric machinery movers use BLDC gear motors (200-2000W) to drive the platform, replacing manual pushing or forklift transport. They handle loads from 1 to 50+ tons depending on the number of drive units."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"How do I calculate the drive motor power for a machinery mover?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"P = (m × g × μ + m × a) × v / η. For a 10-ton load on polyurethane wheels (μ = 0.03) at 0.3 m/s with acceleration 0.1 m/s²: P = (10000 × 9.81 × 0.03 + 10000 × 0.1) × 0.3 / 0.85 = (2943 + 1000) × 0.3 / 0.85 = 1,393W. A dual-motor setup with 2× 750W BLDC motors provides this with safety margin."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"What wheels work best for machinery movers?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Polyurethane wheels are the standard for factory floors: low rolling resistance (μ = 0.02-0.04), no floor marking, good load capacity, and quiet operation. Steel wheels are used for rail-guided systems. Nylon wheels handle extreme loads but have higher rolling resistance and are noisier. Wheel diameter: 100-200mm for movers under 20 tons, 200-300mm for heavier loads."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"Can machinery movers climb ramps?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, but the motor power requirement increases significantly. A 5% gradient adds approximately 490W per ton of load. For a 10-ton machinery mover on a 5% ramp: additional power = 10 × 490 = 4,900W. This typically requires 4 drive motors or a dedicated ramp crawler unit. Most factory floors are level (under 1% grade), so standard dual-motor configurations handle normal use."}},
    {"@type":"Question","name":"What safety features do electric machinery movers need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Essential safety features: emergency stop button (hardwired, not software), deadman switch on the remote control (motor stops when button is released), overcurrent protection (prevents motor damage if the load jams), anti-rollback on slopes (electromagnetic brake or worm gear self-locking), and bumper sensors for collision avoidance. Speed limiting to 0.5 m/s maximum is also recommended for loads over 5 tons."}}
  ]
