<article class="section">
  <div class="container content-narrow">
    <p>Moving heavy machinery inside a factory is one of the most dangerous and time-consuming tasks in manufacturing. Traditional methods — forklifts, cranes, and manual pushing with crowbars — are slow, unsafe, and often damage both the machine and the floor. Electric machinery movers with BLDC drive motors solve all three problems: they move loads of 1-50+ tons at walking speed, under precise remote control, with zero floor damage.</p>

    <h2>Motor Sizing for Different Load Classes</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Load Class</th><th>Typical Load</th><th>Motors</th><th>Total Power</th><th>Speed</th><th>Gear Ratio</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Light (1-5 tons)</td><td>CNC lathes, milling machines</td><td>1-2 × 200-400W</td><td>200-800W</td><td>0.3-0.5 m/s</td><td>20:1-30:1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Medium (5-15 tons)</td><td>Injection molds, stamping presses</td><td>2-4 × 400-750W</td><td>800-3,000W</td><td>0.2-0.4 m/s</td><td>30:1-50:1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Heavy (15-50 tons)</td><td>Die casting machines, transformers</td><td>4-8 × 750-1,500W</td><td>3,000-12,000W</td><td>0.1-0.3 m/s</td><td>40:1-80:1</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Drive Force Calculation</h2>
    <p>The total force the motors must produce:</p>
    <p><strong>F<sub>total</sub> = F<sub>rolling</sub> + F<sub>acceleration</sub> + F<sub>gradient</sub></strong></p>
    <p>Rolling resistance: F<sub>rolling</sub> = m × g × μ. For polyurethane wheels on epoxy-coated concrete: μ = 0.025-0.035. For steel wheels on rail: μ = 0.005-0.010.</p>
    <p>Acceleration force: F<sub>accel</sub> = m × a. Typical acceleration for machinery movers: 0.05-0.15 m/s² (very gentle to prevent load shifting).</p>
    <p>Gradient force: F<sub>gradient</sub> = m × g × sin(α). Even 1% floor slope adds significant force for heavy loads: 10 tons on 1% = 981 N.</p>
    <p><strong>Example — 8-ton injection mold on PU wheels:</strong></p>
    <p>F = 8000 × 9.81 × 0.03 + 8000 × 0.1 = 2,354 + 800 = 3,154 N. At 0.3 m/s, η = 0.85: P = 3,154 × 0.3 / 0.85 = 1,113W. Solution: 2 × 750W BLDC motors with <a href="/blog/bldc-motor-sizing-agv/">planetary gear reducers</a> at 30:1.</p>

    <h2>Remote Control and Speed Management</h2>
    <p>All machinery movers must be operated by remote control — the operator needs a clear view of the load and surrounding area. BLDC motor controllers integrate directly with wireless remote control systems:</p>
    <p><strong>Proportional speed control:</strong> The joystick position maps proportionally to motor speed (0-100%). No sudden starts or stops. Essential for positioning heavy loads with millimeter precision.</p>
    <p><strong>Deadman switch:</strong> The motor runs only while the operator holds a trigger. Releasing stops the motor immediately. This is a safety requirement in all industrial settings.</p>
    <p><strong>Differential steering:</strong> For omnidirectional movers, the controller varies speed between left and right drive motors to achieve turning. Mecanum wheel configurations use 4 independently controlled BLDC motors for true omnidirectional movement (forward, sideways, diagonal, and rotation).</p>

    <h2>Battery Systems</h2>
    <p>Electric machinery movers use rechargeable battery packs, typically 24V or 48V lithium (LiFePO4 for safety). Battery sizing depends on daily usage pattern:</p>
    <p><strong>Intermittent use (2-4 moves per day, 5-10 minutes each):</strong> A 48V 20Ah battery (960Wh) provides 6-10 moves on a single charge for a 2×750W dual-motor system.</p>
    <p><strong>Continuous use (warehouse relocation, factory rearrangement):</strong> A 48V 50-100Ah battery (2,400-4,800Wh) provides 3-5 hours of continuous operation. On-board charger allows overnight charging from standard 220V outlet.</p>

    <h2>Safety Features</h2>
    <p>Moving multi-ton loads creates serious safety risks. Required safety features:</p>
    <p><strong>Emergency stop:</strong> Hardwired (not software-dependent) emergency stop on the remote control and on the mover itself. Cuts power to all motors and engages electromagnetic brake.</p>
    <p><strong>Anti-rollback:</strong> On any slope, the load must not roll when the motor stops. Options: electromagnetic brake on each motor, or worm gear drive with self-locking (no additional brake needed). We recommend the <a href="/blog/bldc-motor-sizing-agv/">worm gear approach</a> for its simplicity and fail-safe nature.</p>
    <p><strong>Overcurrent protection:</strong> If the load jams against an obstacle, the controller limits motor current to prevent motor damage and alerts the operator via the remote display.</p>
    <p><strong>Speed limiting:</strong> Maximum speed of 0.5 m/s for loads over 5 tons, 0.3 m/s for loads over 15 tons. Programmable in the BLDC controller.</p>

